WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:08.960
So the substring can go and extracts a part of a string at a specified position.

00:08.960 --> 00:12.120
So this time we don't want something from the beginning or the end.

00:12.160 --> 00:13.760
We want something like in the middle.

00:13.760 --> 00:18.840
So we want to specify the starting position and we want to extract few characters from there.

00:18.880 --> 00:21.040
So let's have very simple example to understand it.

00:21.040 --> 00:24.080
Now in order to use the substring you need three things.

00:24.080 --> 00:29.040
The first one is the value itself, where you want to extract a specific part from it, and then you

00:29.040 --> 00:34.640
have to specify the starting position where SQL can start extracting the characters that you want.

00:34.680 --> 00:38.520
And as well SQL needs the links, how many characters we have to extract.

00:38.520 --> 00:44.240
So now let's say that we have the following task after the second character extract two characters.

00:44.240 --> 00:48.200
So from reading this you can see we specified the starting position.

00:48.200 --> 00:52.240
This is the second character and the length going to be the two characters.

00:52.240 --> 00:54.320
So let's have this example if we have Mariam.

00:54.320 --> 00:56.600
So now we have to specify the starting position.

00:56.600 --> 00:59.120
Now we are saying after the second character.

00:59.120 --> 01:02.210
So the first character M is one, then A is two.

01:02.250 --> 01:04.570
After two we got the position number three.

01:04.610 --> 01:05.010
Right.

01:05.010 --> 01:06.490
So starting from R.

01:06.610 --> 01:11.810
So that means we have to specify for SQL three because the starting position can be number three.

01:11.850 --> 01:12.930
This is after the two.

01:12.930 --> 01:14.890
Now we want only two characters.

01:14.890 --> 01:16.730
So we want the R and the I.

01:16.770 --> 01:23.290
If you give this to SQL Maria starting position three and the links to SQL can go and extract the two

01:23.290 --> 01:24.890
characters the r I.

01:25.010 --> 01:26.250
And this is exactly what you want.

01:26.250 --> 01:30.050
We want two characters after the second position the second character.

01:30.050 --> 01:33.410
So with that we didn't extract something from the left or from the right.

01:33.410 --> 01:35.970
We extracted at specific position.

01:35.970 --> 01:38.130
And this is exactly why we need the substring.

01:38.130 --> 01:43.410
Now let's make it a little bit more difficult where we're going to say after the second character extract

01:43.410 --> 01:45.090
everything, all the characters.

01:45.090 --> 01:47.690
So not only r I, I would like Aria.

01:47.850 --> 01:49.890
So now nothing's changed about the starting position.

01:49.890 --> 01:51.090
It's going to stay at three.

01:51.130 --> 01:56.290
But now if you are looking to this value and you want to extract everything starting from R, that means

01:56.290 --> 01:58.490
you have to specify the length of three.

01:58.530 --> 02:02.370
But this is not really good because let's have another value in the same column.

02:02.370 --> 02:03.450
So we have Martin.

02:03.450 --> 02:05.490
So the starting position can be as well.

02:05.530 --> 02:05.850
Ah.

02:06.010 --> 02:07.930
And now the link is going to be different.

02:07.930 --> 02:10.010
So we have here four characters.

02:10.010 --> 02:11.770
So now the links is not anymore three.

02:11.810 --> 02:12.490
It is four.

02:12.490 --> 02:14.210
But you have to specify something at the end.

02:14.210 --> 02:16.090
For SQL you can go for four.

02:16.130 --> 02:17.890
That's fine for Maria as well.

02:17.890 --> 02:22.850
But if you have a lot of values, it's going to be really hard to specify exactly the correct links.

02:22.850 --> 02:28.570
That's why instead of specifying a static number like 3 or 4, we can use another function.

02:28.570 --> 02:33.850
So now, my friends, if you use the links function you will get the total number of characters right.

02:33.850 --> 02:35.810
So for Maria you will get five.

02:35.850 --> 02:37.530
For Martin you will get six.

02:37.530 --> 02:41.570
And those numbers are okay to use in the links because they are more than what we need.

02:41.610 --> 02:42.770
And that's totally fine.

02:42.770 --> 02:48.490
So if you are saying okay for Maria, start from the third position and cut for me, five characters

02:48.530 --> 02:51.210
is going to find only three, but you will not get an error.

02:51.250 --> 02:56.010
So you are extracting more than you need and you will always get all the characters after the starting

02:56.010 --> 02:56.610
position.

02:56.610 --> 03:01.850
So this is a little trick that we use in order to make the links dynamic, where we cannot find one

03:01.850 --> 03:04.620
value that we can use in all scenarios.

03:04.660 --> 03:08.300
And now let's go back to SQL in order to practice that substring.

03:08.340 --> 03:08.580
Okay.

