So now we have the following task.

03:09.900 --> 03:15.260
And it says retrieve a list of customers first names after removing the first character.

03:15.260 --> 03:20.020
So now don't ask me why, but for some reason we don't want to see the first character of the first

03:20.060 --> 03:20.500
names.

03:20.500 --> 03:21.620
We want to remove it.

03:21.620 --> 03:22.740
So how we can do that?

03:22.740 --> 03:24.420
We cannot use the left or the right.

03:24.420 --> 03:28.500
We have to go with the substring because it is a little bit more complicated.

03:28.900 --> 03:33.300
So substring and let's go and get and the first argument is going to be the value.

03:33.300 --> 03:34.820
So it comes from the first name.

03:34.820 --> 03:37.540
And then the second argument is the starting position.

03:37.540 --> 03:43.380
So where we want to start since it is saying I want all the characters after the first character.

03:43.380 --> 03:46.820
So that means we will be starting from the position number two.

03:46.860 --> 03:50.380
So for example Maria here, the first character m position number one.

03:50.380 --> 03:53.820
And we want to start our substring from the position number two.

03:53.860 --> 03:55.220
So this is really simple.

03:55.220 --> 03:56.220
So that's what.

03:56.260 --> 03:57.900
So that was the easy part.

03:57.940 --> 04:02.140
Now the next one the question is how much characters we want to leave.

04:02.340 --> 04:05.630
So do we leave here like four characters like Maria.

04:05.630 --> 04:09.030
We have four characters, but in John we have only three.

04:09.070 --> 04:11.230
Then the next one is four and so on.

04:11.230 --> 04:18.670
So if you go for example with four and let's call it sub name, so we make it static, what can happen.

04:18.670 --> 04:24.830
It's going to work for some scenarios like Maria we have here Aria and for bitter we are getting Iter.

04:24.990 --> 04:27.030
But for Martin it is not working.

04:27.030 --> 04:31.750
We are not getting the last N because it has like five characters after the first one.

04:31.750 --> 04:36.230
And by just looking at the result, as you can see, we have here one issue with John, and that's because

04:36.230 --> 04:38.630
the first character is an empty string.

04:38.630 --> 04:40.230
So this is really annoying.

04:40.230 --> 04:45.470
So that's why we use the trim first just to get rid of all those white spaces.

04:45.510 --> 04:47.590
And now you can see it's working fine.

04:47.590 --> 04:49.230
So we are not getting the G.

04:49.270 --> 04:50.990
We have everything after the first character.

04:50.990 --> 04:55.270
So now instead of having this static what we're going to do we're going to make it variable.

04:55.270 --> 04:59.350
So we're going to go and use the length of the first name.

04:59.990 --> 05:04.150
So with that we make sure we have enough length to extract.

05:04.150 --> 05:09.470
And this can work for any value inside the first name, even if the name is like 20 characters.

05:09.470 --> 05:11.430
So let's go and execute.

05:11.470 --> 05:14.190
And now you can see for Martin it is now working.

05:14.190 --> 05:19.550
So we have here like five characters after the M and here we have four characters after the M as well.

05:19.550 --> 05:21.550
And here we have three characters after the G.

05:21.710 --> 05:25.430
So it is working completely and it is full dynamic.

05:25.430 --> 05:29.110
So this is the trick by using the links together with the substring.

05:29.110 --> 05:32.630
And as you can see now we are using three functions in one go.

05:32.670 --> 05:35.670
We have the links, we have the trim and we have the substring.

05:35.670 --> 05:37.350
And this is what happens in SQL.

05:37.350 --> 05:41.990
We use multiple functions together in order to solve like complex tasks.

05:41.990 --> 05:46.070
So this is how you can extract a substring from a string.

